{"task_id": "Mbpp/109", "solution": "def odd_Equivalent(s: str, times: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the number of times a binary string, when rotated, results in an odd integer.\n    Rotations are considered from 0 up to (times - 1) right circular shifts.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n\n    if times == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # Count total '1's in the string. An 'odd' number means its LSB is '1'.\n    # In a binary string, this is the last character.\n    total_ones_in_s = s.count('1')\n\n    # Calculate full cycles of rotations\n    full_cycles = times // n\n    \n    # Calculate remaining rotations (less than one full cycle)\n    remainder_rotations = times % n\n\n    # The base count comes from how many full cycles occurred.\n    # In each full cycle, every '1' in the original string will appear at the LSB exactly once.\n    count = full_cycles * total_ones_in_s\n\n    # For the remainder rotations, manually check which character lands at the LSB.\n    for i in range(remainder_rotations):\n        # Determine the original index that moves to the LSB (last position, index n-1)\n        # after 'i' right circular shifts.\n        # The LSB after 'i' right rotations corresponds to the character at original index (n - 1 - i) % n.\n        original_idx_at_lsb = (n - 1 - i) % n\n        \n        if s[original_idx_at_lsb] == '1':\n            count += 1\n            \n    return count"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/113", "solution": "def check_integer(s):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a string represents an integer, considering ASCII digits only.\n    Leading/trailing whitespace and a single leading sign (+ or -) are allowed.\n    \"\"\"\n    s_stripped = s.strip()\n\n    # Empty string or string with only whitespace is not an integer.\n    if not s_stripped:\n        return False\n\n    # Determine the actual digit part, excluding a potential leading sign.\n    digits_part = s_stripped\n    if s_stripped[0] == '+' or s_stripped[0] == '-':\n        digits_part = s_stripped[1:]\n\n    # A string that is just a sign (e.g., \"+\" or \"-\") is not an integer.\n    if not digits_part:\n        return False\n\n    # Validate that all characters in the digit part are ASCII digits.\n    # This ensures unicode numerals are not accepted, as specified in the design.\n    allowed_digits = \"0123456789\"\n    for char in digits_part:\n        if char not in allowed_digits:\n            return False\n\n    # Attempt final conversion using Python's built-in int() for robustness.\n    # This catches cases like \"007\" which are valid, and would fail if only char check was done.\n    try:\n        int(s_stripped)\n        return True\n    except ValueError:\n        # If int() raises a ValueError, the string is not a valid integer.\n        return False"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/111", "solution": "from typing import List, Any, Set, Union\n\ndef common_in_nested_lists(nested_lists: List[List[Any]], as_set: bool = False) -> Union[List[Any], Set[Any]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the common elements across all given nested lists.\n    Returns a set if `as_set` is True, otherwise a list preserving order from the first inner list.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not nested_lists:\n        return set() if as_set else []\n\n    common_elements_set = set(nested_lists[0])\n\n    if not common_elements_set and not nested_lists[0]: # handle case where first list is empty but common_elements_set is still created.\n        return set() if as_set else []\n\n    for inner_list in nested_lists[1:]:\n        common_elements_set.intersection_update(set(inner_list))\n        if not common_elements_set:\n            return set() if as_set else []\n\n    if as_set:\n        return common_elements_set\n    else:\n        # Reconstruct the list preserving the order of appearance from the first list\n        ordered_result = [item for item in nested_lists[0] if item in common_elements_set]\n        return ordered_result"}
